The `IOUtils.skip` method is supposed to skip a specified number of bytes from an input stream and return the actual number of bytes skipped, handling the case where the end of the input stream has been reached. However, the implementation of the `skip` method directly calls `input.skip(numToSkip)` and breaks the loop if `skip` returns 0, which is not in accordance with the contract.

Here is the relevant code snippet of the `IOUtils.skip` method:

```java
     public static long skip(InputStream input, long numToSkip) throws IOException {
         long available = numToSkip;
         while (numToSkip > 0) {
             long skipped = input.skip(numToSkip);
             if (skipped == 0) {
                 break;
             }
             numToSkip -= skipped;
         }

         return available - numToSkip;
     }
```

The issue seems to be with how the `skip` method handles the case when `skip` returns 0. Instead of breaking the loop immediately, it should continue and try to skip more bytes until the specified number of bytes has been skipped or the end of the stream is reached.

To fix this issue, the `IOUtils.skip` method should be modified to correctly handle the case when `skip` returns 0 and continue skipping bytes until the desired number of bytes have been skipped.

